// Single source of truth for project descriptions.
// Rendered on the Research page and inside the CV's publication hover tooltips,
// so editing a description here updates both places at once.
export const publicationDescriptions = {
  transfer: `A transfer experiment is often used to infer what a learner acquired: a rule, memories of specific examples, or reusable components. I test whether a design can actually distinguish those possibilities before data are collected. Each account is formalised as a small family of learners defined by two dimensions: how the stimulus is represented, and how that representation is converted into a response. The learners generate predictions for every test item. When two different mechanisms produce the same predictions, the experiment cannot separate them regardless of sample size. The framework is released as the open-source Python toolkit tide, together with a web interface.`,

  encoding: `Why can a learner demonstrate a rule during training yet fail to use it on a new item? We separate transfer into two representational questions. Functional encoding asks whether a useful component enters the representation that drives the response. Representational binding asks whether that component remains available as a reusable part or becomes fused with the whole stimulus. One model, held fixed across artificial orthography, Chinese character learning, and artificial grammar, shows that tests preserving a trained component primarily probe encoding, while tests recombining components probe binding. A continuous binding parameter accounts for patterns that earlier theories attributed to two separate systems.`,

  smoothLearningCurve: `Why do some learning curves rise abruptly while others change gradually? I propose that the curve reflects both the learning process and the format of what is being learned. When the environment supports a definite structure that can be identified, evidence can produce a sharp commitment. When the learner must estimate a probability distribution, behaviour changes more gradually. This places apparently abrupt rule learning and smooth statistical learning on a common axis determined by the predictability of the next event. I also analyse when the shape of an individual learner's trajectory is recoverable from noisy data, showing that many existing designs are too sparse to identify it reliably.`,

  fittedAssistance: `When a writer is still discovering what they think, AI can return a polished structure that fits the available notes so well that the inquiry appears settled. This project asks what cognitive work may be displaced by that convenience. Developing a structure can require assembling the support that makes it intelligible, comparing it with alternatives already within reach, and widening the inquiry until new possibilities appear. Fitted assistance may shorten each process, leading respectively to shallow uptake, route shaping, and foreclosed reach. The consequences depend on whether the task requires the writer to command an existing structure, exercise judgment over its organisation, or discover something through writing. The design question is therefore not simply how much assistance to provide, but when it enters and what thinking remains with the writer.`,

  humansDisengage: `Reasoning models and people both take longer on harder problems, so their aggregate behaviour can look similar. Within individual items, however, the relationship reverses. When a model answers incorrectly, it typically spends more tokens than when it answers the same item correctly. People show the opposite pattern, responding fastest on the trials they ultimately fail. The same difficulty trend comes from different control policies. Models persist when uncertain, whereas people are more likely to disengage.`,

  priorsPersist: `If a language model is told that, within a document, <i>doctor</i> means <i>forest</i>, it can answer <i>forest</i> when queried. The question is whether the established <i>doctor</i> &rarr; <i>hospital</i> association has been removed or merely overridden. Across 11 open-weight models, the prior association continues to influence behaviour, with stronger original associations producing more leakage. Causal analysis shows that the override depends jointly on the definition's subject, its new target, and the queried word. Successful override is supported by maintaining the contextual target, rather than by suppressing the prior association itself.`,

  functionVector: `When a language model infers a task from examples in its prompt, a small set of attention heads is often credited with carrying the corresponding function vector. The standard method ranks heads by the magnitude of their effect on the answer. I show that this combines two functionally opposite populations. Writer heads push the model toward the rule-consistent answer, while canceller heads push against it. Both can have large effects, but a magnitude-only ranking does not reveal the distinction and can obscure one side of the mechanism.`,

  bright: `Most retrieval benchmarks can be solved largely through keyword or surface-semantic matching. BRIGHT tests whether a retriever can identify documents whose relevance becomes clear only after reasoning about the query. The benchmark contains 1,384 real queries across economics, psychology, mathematics, and coding. Strong retrievers that perform well on conventional benchmarks decline sharply on BRIGHT, while adding explicit query reasoning improves retrieval and downstream question answering. The results show that reasoning-intensive retrieval remains a substantially different challenge from ordinary semantic matching.`,
};
