<template>
  <div>
    <p class="back-link"><router-link :to="{ path: '/projects', query: { to: 'insurance' } }">&larr; Back to projects</router-link></p>
    <h1 data-scroll-reveal>Insurance Product Configuration Engine</h1>

    <article data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p class="lede">
        Packing for a trip follows a set of conditional rules, even if we rarely describe it that way. A beach trip
        calls for swimwear. Water sports add a snorkel. A ski trip produces a different list. A few answers determine
        what belongs in the bag.
      </p>
      <p>
        At <strong>HSBC</strong> I built an engine for a much larger version of the same problem. The interactive
        example below uses packing rules instead of confidential insurance data, but the underlying logic is the same.
        First it shows the problem. Then it lets you operate the rule system yourself.
      </p>

      <!-- 1 · THE PROBLEM -->
      <h2>First, the problem</h2>
      <p>
        One banking app supports many insurance products, and each product requires a different combination of screens
        and fields. A travel policy asks for trip dates, a motor policy asks about the vehicle, and some fields appear
        only after an earlier answer makes them relevant. The same logic must work across dozens of products and
        multiple markets.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rules were spread across business requirements, design libraries, and engineering implementation. Each
        team understood one part, but there was no shared representation connecting a product specification to the
        screens the app should display. As the sources changed, they could drift out of sync.
      </p>

      <!-- 2 · THE IDEA -->
      <h2>The idea</h2>
      <p>I separated the information into three linked libraries, with each part maintained by the team closest to it.</p>
      <div class="lib-cards" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="120">
        <div class="lib-card">
          <span class="lib-tag">Spec</span>
          <p>What information does the product require? The business fields and their allowed values.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lib-card">
          <span class="lib-tag">Components</span>
          <p>What can the interface display? The reusable screens and UI components.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lib-card">
          <span class="lib-tag">Config</span>
          <p>Which components apply under each set of conditions? The decision tables connecting specifications to interface blocks.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Keeping these libraries separate allowed business rules and interface components to change independently. The
        configuration tables connected them at runtime. Given a product's answers, the engine selected the required
        components.
      </p>

      <!-- 3 · THE PLAYGROUND -->
      <h2>Try it</h2>
      <p class="play-intro">
        This demo uses the same logic with a familiar example. Describe the trip, watch each decision table evaluate
        the answers, and see the final packing list assemble itself.
      </p>

      <div class="playground" data-scroll-pop="true">

        <!-- STEP 1 — answer -->
        <section class="stage">
          <header class="stage-head"><span class="stage-num">1</span> Tell us about the trip</header>
          <div class="qform">
            <label v-for="f in visible" :key="f.sno" class="qrow" :class="{ 'is-sub': f.parent }">
              <span class="qlabel">
                <span v-if="f.parent" class="sub-arrow">↳</span>{{ f.name }}
              </span>
              <select v-model="answers[f.sno]" class="qselect">
                <option v-for="p in f.params" :key="p" :value="p">{{ p }}</option>
              </select>
            </label>
          </div>
          <p class="stage-note" v-if="hasSub">
            The follow-up question appears only when an earlier answer makes it relevant. In the production tool, the
            dependency was encoded in the field definition, allowing the form to update without requiring users to
            edit the VBA logic.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- STEP 2 — rules react, one table per decision -->
        <section class="stage">
          <header class="stage-head"><span class="stage-num">2</span> Each decision gets its own rule table</header>
          <p class="stage-note stage-note--top">
            Each table represents one decision. A column remains eligible only when all of its conditions match the
            answers. If several columns match, the most specific column on the right takes priority. ALL acts as a
            wildcard.
          </p>

          <div v-for="group in ev.groups" :key="group.cfg.id" class="cfg">
            <div class="cfg-head">
              <span class="cfg-name">{{ group.cfg.name }}</span>
              <span class="cfg-blurb">{{ group.cfg.blurb }}</span>
            </div>
            <div class="table-scroll">
              <table class="rules">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th class="rowhead"></th>
                    <th
                      v-for="(s, j) in group.cfg.scenarios" :key="s.name"
                      class="schead"
                      :class="{ matched: group.results[j].match, winning: group.winning === j }"
                    >{{ s.name }}</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="group-row"><td :colspan="group.cfg.scenarios.length + 1">Your answers (inputs)</td></tr>
                  <tr v-for="sno in group.cfg.inputs" :key="sno" :class="{ dim: !ev.visible.has(sno) }">
                    <td class="rowhead">{{ fname(sno) }}</td>
                    <td
                      v-for="(s, j) in group.cfg.scenarios" :key="s.name"
                      class="cell spec-cell"
                      :class="{ colmatch: group.results[j].match, winning: group.winning === j, bad: !group.results[j].cell[sno].ok }"
                    >{{ group.results[j].cell[sno].req }}</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="group-row"><td :colspan="group.cfg.scenarios.length + 1">What to pack (outputs)</td></tr>
                  <tr v-for="id in group.cfg.items" :key="id">
                    <td class="rowhead">{{ iname(id) }}</td>
                    <td
                      v-for="(s, j) in group.cfg.scenarios" :key="s.name"
                      class="cell comp-cell"
                      :class="[ s.out[id] === 'Y' ? 'yes' : 'no', { colmatch: group.results[j].match, winning: group.winning === j } ]"
                    >{{ s.out[id] }}</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p class="cfg-note">
              <template v-if="matchCount(group) > 1">
                Your answers match {{ matchCount(group) }} columns. The most specific,
                <strong>“{{ winnerName(group) }}”</strong>, wins.
              </template>
              <template v-else>
                Your answers land on <strong>“{{ winnerName(group) }}”</strong>.
              </template>
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- STEP 3 — the bag packs itself -->
        <section class="stage">
          <header class="stage-head"><span class="stage-num">3</span> The bag packs itself</header>
          <div class="phone-wrap">
            <div class="phone">
              <div class="phone-notch"></div>
              <div class="phone-screen">
                <div class="app-bar">
                  <span>Packing list</span>
                  <span class="app-count">{{ ev.shown.length }}</span>
                </div>
                <div class="app-body">
                  <div v-for="c in ev.shown" :key="c.id" class="app-card">
                    <span class="app-check" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <span class="app-card-text">
                      <span class="app-card-title">{{ c.name }}</span>
                      <span class="app-card-hint">{{ c.hint }}</span>
                    </span>
                  </div>
                  <div v-if="!ev.shown.length" class="app-empty">
                    Just the essentials for this trip.
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="phone-side">
              <p class="phone-caption">
                The same inputs produce the same output every time. Each decision table contributes part of the
                result, and the combined output becomes the final list. The rules remain explicit in the tables
                instead of relying on someone to remember them.
              </p>
              <div v-for="group in ev.groups" :key="group.cfg.id" class="legend-group">
                <span class="legend-title">{{ group.cfg.name }}</span>
                <ul class="comp-legend">
                  <li v-for="id in group.cfg.items" :key="id" :class="ev.out[id] === 'Y' ? 'on' : 'off'">
                    <span class="dot"></span>{{ iname(id) }}
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>

      <!-- 4 · WHAT IT REALLY WAS -->
      <h2>What this really was</h2>
      <p>
        The production version ran in Excel with a VBA engine of roughly 32 KB. It included a small notation for
        conditional follow-up fields, decision tables with wildcard matching and right-side overrides, and a dynamic
        form that non-technical users could complete without editing the underlying code. I developed four versions
        and added an embedded tutorial so another team member could maintain the tool after my internship.
      </p>
      <p>
        The packing demo replaces confidential insurance data, but the mechanism is the same. It converts fragmented
        business logic into a shared representation that people can inspect and a computer can execute.
      </p>
      <p class="back-link"><router-link :to="{ path: '/projects', query: { to: 'insurance' } }">← Back to projects</router-link></p>
    </article>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
import scrollRevealMixin from '../mixins/scrollRevealMixin';
import { fields, components, configurators, visibleFields, evaluate, defaultAnswers, fieldBySno, componentById } from '../data/playbookDemo';

export default {
  name: 'PlaybookTab',
  mixins: [scrollRevealMixin],
  data() {
    return {
      fields,
      components,
      configurators,
      answers: defaultAnswers(),
    };
  },
  computed: {
    visible() { return visibleFields(this.answers); },
    hasSub() { return this.visible.some(f => f.parent); },
    ev() { return evaluate(this.answers); },
  },
  methods: {
    fname(sno) { const f = fieldBySno(sno); return f ? f.name : sno; },
    iname(id) { const c = componentById(id); return c ? c.name : id; },
    matchCount(group) { return group.results.filter(r => r.match).length; },
    winnerName(group) { return group.winning >= 0 ? group.cfg.scenarios[group.winning].name : '—'; },
  },
};
</script>

