<template>
  <div class="project-story scrolly-story">
    <p class="back-link"><router-link :to="{ path: '/projects', query: { to: 'scrollytelling' } }">&larr; Back to projects</router-link></p>
    <h1 data-scroll-reveal>Scrollytelling Engine</h1>

    <article>
      <p class="lede">
        A course project required me to explain a data-heavy topic through maps, distributions, and flows. On static
        slides, the relationships were difficult to follow because the audience had to absorb the chart and the
        argument at the same time. I therefore built the presentation as a scrolling web narrative. Each visual could
        remain in place while the surrounding explanation changed. As I developed the deck, I separated the
        presentation system from the original topic and turned it into a reusable engine.
      </p>

      <h2>A scene is just data</h2>
      <p>
        Each example below is rendered by the same builders used in the full deck. A scene specifies a figure type and
        a small set of properties. The registry selects the matching builder, renders the figure, and applies its
        entrance animation. Choose a chart type to see the schema and the result together.
      </p>

      <div class="explorer" data-scroll-pop="true">
        <div class="cs-chips">
          <button
            v-for="c in charts"
            :key="c.key"
            class="cs-chip"
            :class="{ active: c.key === selectedChart }"
            @click="select(c.key)"
          >{{ c.label }}</button>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-stage" ref="stage"></div>
        <div class="cs-meta">
          <p class="cs-blurb">{{ current.blurb }}</p>
          <pre class="cs-code"><code>{{ current.schema }}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="cs-foot">
        Once a builder exists in the registry, most new scenes can be added through a small data object rather than
        new rendering code.
      </p>

      <h2>See it as a story</h2>
      <p>
        The examples above show the engine's visual vocabulary. The embedded deck below is the course presentation for
        which the system was originally built. Scroll inside the frame to see how fixed visuals, changing text, and
        animated data work together across a full narrative.
      </p>
      <div class="deck-embed" data-scroll-pop="true">
        <div class="deck-frame" ref="deckFrame">
          <iframe ref="deckIframe" src="https://www.henryw.me/CCGL9074/" title="Live scrollytelling deck built on the engine" loading="lazy"></iframe>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="cs-foot">
        Cramped in the frame? <a href="https://www.henryw.me/CCGL9074/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open it fullscreen &nearr;</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Under the hood</h2>
      <div class="feature-list">
        <div class="feature-row">
          <span>Declarative slides</span>
          <p>Each scene is defined as an object containing its section background, navigation label, and figures. Shared tokens control layout, typography, and reveal timing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-row">
          <span>A figure registry</span>
          <p>A figure's type selects a builder for text, line charts, ridge plots, scatter plots, heatmaps, geographic maps, Sankey diagrams, chord diagrams, and other formats.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-row">
          <span>Background manager</span>
          <p>Each section can use its own looping video layer, managed separately from the scene markup.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-row">
